## Landed semantics

- F-249 **Under walk, a program that imports only the prelude reaches the string objects' own `||` only through `String`'s abstract `opr ||(self, b:String)`.** The string objects are `FlatString`, `Concatenable` and `EmptyString`. Code inside `FortressLibrary` reaches `FlatString`'s own `||` directly, because `FortressLibrary` imports `FlatString`'s api, which declares it. This bears on one proposed change: to move `String`'s `||` members to top-level declarations over `(Any, String)` and `(String, Any)`, as the library declares `juxtaposition`. Their meet, the top-level declaration over `(String, String)` that the Meet Rule requires, must then delegate to `||` declarations that an imported api states. Otherwise every concatenation runs the meet's own body: with a body of its own, `"s" || "t"` gives a `CatString` of depth 1, where the library today gives a `FlatString` of depth 0, with the same printed value. Observed once, in probes on a patched library; no gated test holds it. Detail: `perf-probes/symbolic-operators/REPORT.md` section 2.4.
  by: the clarity pass; note: rewritten for a reader without the probe (consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/facts/f14/proposals.md, "F-249"); the title scoped to a program that imports only the prelude, as section 2.4 states it, with FortressLibrary's own direct route; "juxtaposition's device" said in plain words and the pair that the meet covers named (section 2.2, ways JC and JCp); the kind of evidence said: one probe run on a patched library (way JCp)
- F-252 **Under walk, where the parameter-by-parameter check refuses two declarations with static parameters of their own, the load check reads the pair again on its declared domains, by the No Duplicates, Return Type and Meet Rules.** A declaration with static parameters of its own is a generic function, a generic object's constructor, or a functional method that declares static parameters in a trait or object that has none. Walk's load check first compares two overloads parameter by parameter, and a pair that it finds excluding loads. It reads a refused pair of two such declarations again, each over all its own instances, and loads the pair if it meets the three rules. F-?1 holds the refusal of equal declared domains, and F-?2 the Return Type Rule. The parameter check does not find two instances of one generic type at different ground static arguments excluding, such as `Tg[\3\]` and `Tg[\4\]` (row 416). For the Meet Rule, walk looks for a third declaration that covers the overlap only where, at each position, the two parameter types are instances of one generic type or static parameters (row 539); a pair whose domains exclude when read whole also loads (F-258). A pair with a varargs parameter, or with parameter lists of different lengths, is not read again and keeps the parameter check's refusal (F-257). At a call, walk compares a functional method of a generic trait or object on the instance that the call makes, and a generic dotted method the same way, except where exactly one of the two declared domains lies inside the other (F-253). Gated by `tests/DispatchRenamedGenericArmWalk`, `DispatchSwappedGenericArmWalk` and `GenericPairNestedBoundsWalk` (load and dispatch), and the expected failure `XXXOverloadExistentialMeetWalk` (row 539). Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-walk-generic-pairs/REPORT.md` sections 1 and 5.
  by: the clarity pass; note: reorganized (consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/facts/f14/proposals.md, "F-252"); split into this entry, F-?1 (equal declared domains, No Duplicates) and F-?2 (the Return Type Rule at every instance), which change apart; "own generic" said in plain words from ownGeneric's comment (OverloadedFunction.java, rung-existential-reduction/REPORT.md section 1); the title scoped to the pairs that the parameter check refuses, and the pairs not read (varargs, different lengths) stated, from genericPairValid (OverloadedFunction.java:897); the row 416 limit placed on the parameter check, where it holds; moved here: the code sites genericPairValid (cited as :766, which is validOnDeclaredDomains; genericPairValid is at :894 now), :533-540 and sameDeclaredDomains :806 at 6d187d069, returnRuleAtEveryInstance at 36afa7eb1
- F-?1 **Under walk, two declarations whose declared domains are equal are refused by the No Duplicates Rule, where one has static parameters of its own and the other has its own or none, whether or not the parameter-by-parameter check orders them.** The parameter check reads a type parameter as strictly below its bound. So it orders `h[\T extends Number\](x: T, y: T)` before `h(x: Number, y: Number)`, though their declared domains are equal. Walk compares the declared domains, each declaration's over all its own instances, and refuses the pair: "fails because their parameter lists have the same types" (F-257). A type parameter bounded by `Any` counts: walk refuses `g[\T\](a: T)` beside `g(a: Any)`, and the compiled checker accepts that pair (row 759). Gated by `tests/GenericBesideGenericSameDomain`, `GenericBesidePlainSameDomain`, `NoDuplicatesRuleWalk` and `NoDuplicatesRuleSizeWalk` (refused). Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-walk-generic-pairs/REPORT.md` sections 1 and 5; `compile-ladder/rung-existential-reduction/REPORT.md` section 5 (row 759 on both paths).
  from: explorations/compile-ladder/rung-walk-generic-pairs/REPORT.md, "1. What changed and why"; by: the clarity pass; note: split from F-252 (consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/facts/f14/proposals.md, "F-?1"); the example h from section 5's table; NoDuplicatesRuleWalk and NoDuplicatesRuleSizeWalk, which F-257 already names, added as gates of the refusal of a pair that the parameter check refuses (genericPairValid's NO_DUPLICATES)
- F-?2 **Under walk, where one of two declarations with static parameters of their own lies inside the other, the load check reads the Return Type Rule at one instance of the outer one, and refuses the pair unless that instance stands for every instance that applies.** The instance is the outer declaration's instance that holds the inner one's declared domain. A check at that instance alone would load pairs that the rule refuses, since an argument of the inner one can select a smaller instance of the outer one. The instance stands for every applicable instance in two cases. In the first, each static parameter that the outer one's return type names is fixed by a static argument of one of its parameter types. In the second, the outer one returns one of its own type parameters, and the inner one either returns a type parameter of its own that is the type of no other parameter, or has, at a position whose type is that returned type parameter, an object type below which its return type lies. Otherwise walk refuses the pair, "fails the Return Type Rule" (F-257), as for `f[\T extends Shape\](x: T, y: T): T` beside `f[\U extends Round\](x: U, y: Round): Round`, and `f[\T extends Shape\](x: T): Box[\T\]` beside `f[\U extends Round\](x: U): Box[\U\]`; the compiled checker refuses both too. Gated by `tests/GenericReturnRuleOuterParamWalk` and `GenericReturnRuleInvariantWalk` (refused) and `GenericReturnRuleHoldsWalk` (load and dispatch). Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-walk-generic-pairs/SKEPTIC.md` section 2.
  from: explorations/compile-ladder/rung-walk-generic-pairs/SKEPTIC.md, "2. The fix: the Return Type Rule at one instance (a defect of the change)"; by: the clarity pass; note: split from F-252 (consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/facts/f14/proposals.md, "F-?2"); the reason for the every-instance condition from the skeptic's finding; the code site returnRuleAtEveryInstance at 36afa7eb1 left to that section
- F-253 **Under walk, a call of a generic method that writes no static argument infers the method's own static arguments from the arguments' run-time types.** This holds for a dotted call, as for a call of a generic function, whether the method is alone under its name or among its receiver's overloads. Among overloads, walk compares a generic dotted method with another on its declared domain where exactly one of the two declared domains lies inside the other. A generic method that a trait declares reads its bounds, and the types to which its arguments convert, where its instances evaluate their types. So it takes the instance that the same method declared in an object takes: a `ZZ32` and a `ZZ64` give `Box[\ZZ64\]` with the `ZZ32` converted, and a bounded type parameter that nothing fixes takes its bound. A generic functional method that an object inherits from a trait is chosen beside the object's plain one where only it applies. Where the inference with conversion finds no instance, as for an argument outside a bound, walk falls back to unification. For a trait's method, that fallback still reads the trait's member environment, as the team's code did, and fails with an error that reads no bound ('Cannot unify Int ... with Shape ... abm=K=(Int,Any)'); the same method declared in an object fails with 'Meet(Int, Shape) not a singleton'. This inference lets the team's generic `distribute` over `K` run nested reductions under walk (F-248). Gated by `tests/MethodStaticArgInference.fss`, `MethodStaticArgOverloadedReceiver.fss`, `MethodStaticArgDeclaredDomain.fss`, `NestedReductionFusion.fss`, `TraitGenericMethodInference.fss` and `InheritedGenericFunctionalMethodBesidePlain.fss`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-walk-method-sargs/REPORT.md` sections 1 and 2; `compile-ladder/rung-walk-trait-method-bounds/REPORT.md` sections 1 and 4, and its `SKEPTIC.md` section 3.
