- F-259 **On both paths, an object or a trait whose `extends` clause names only `Any` is an `Object`; under walk, so is such an object expression.** The pre-disambiguation desugaring, which both paths run, gives such a clause `{Object}`, as it gives an empty clause. It leaves the declarations of `Any` and `Object` themselves as written. So `typecase` takes the `Object` arm for such a value, `cast[\Object\]` returns it, and the value has `Object`'s members, such as `asString`. With this desugaring and the library's conversion (F-?3), the team test `extendAny` prints `Infinity`. Gated by `tests/ExtendsAnyIsObjectWalk.fss`, `tests/ConcatAnyOnLeftWalk.fss` and `compiler_tests/ExtendsAnyIsObject.test`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-string-conversion/REPORT.md` sections 1 and 6.
  by: the clarity pass; note: reorganized (consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/facts/f14/proposals.md, "F-259"); split: the conversion in String's two || members over Any, a walk-only claim, is now F-?3; the object expression scoped to walk (the compiled test leaves it out, a code-generator wall: section 6); FlatString's api line dropped here, since F-246 holds it; moved here: the distance's three String sites gone, 34 to 31, and the checker count 30 to 28, one measurement each on 7ba1a846d (section 4; F-246 measured the same change as 49 to 46 on the probe's base); the distance stage as a gate
- F-?3 **Under walk, `String`'s two `||` members over `Any` convert the other operand without calling `asString` on an `Any`, so a tuple or `()` concatenates on the left of a string.** In the interpreter's library, `opr ||(a:Any, self)` is `("" || a) || self`, and `opr ||(self, b:Any)` is `self || cast[\Object\](b).asString`. So `(1,2) || "s"` gives `(1,2)s`, and `() || "s"` gives `()s`. A function, or a tuple of seven or more elements, beside a string raises `CastError` (the expected failures `XXXFnRenderRungS` and `XXXTupleSevenRungS`, rows 374 and 369). The compiled path links its own prelude, so the conversion does not reach it. F-246 holds the conversion's effect on the distance. Gated by `tests/ConcatAnyOnLeftWalk.fss`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-string-conversion/REPORT.md` sections 1 and 5.
  from: explorations/compile-ladder/rung-string-conversion/REPORT.md, "5. Old against new"; by: the clarity pass; note: split from F-259 (consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/facts/f14/proposals.md, "F-?3"); the path named for the concatenation results; the two expected failures and their rows from section 5
## The library's arrays and algebra

- F-154 **The library declares its matrix and vector products with shared `nat`s, so under walk a product of the wrong shapes stops the run at dispatch.** A shared `nat` is a size parameter that two parameter types name, as `m` in `DOT` and juxtaposition of `Matrix[\T,n,m\]` by `Matrix[\T,m,p\]`. The matrix-vector and vector-matrix products share theirs in the same way. Each product name is overloaded, so the stop reads "Failed to find any matching overload", with the argument types and their sizes. It is a `ProgramError` that no `catch` takes. A declaration that is not overloaded, such as the APL model's `pick`, fails instead with a unification error that names the size; no library product is such a declaration. Observed under walk for a mismatched `DOT` (row 254), and in a probe of the model's own sized declarations, where a `try` around a mismatched call caught nothing. Detail: `apl/mg/NOTES-swap.md`, "What the shared nats catch: the mismatch probe".
  by: the clarity pass; note: reworded (consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/facts/f14/proposals.md, "F-154"); each message tied to the declarations it was observed at: the unification error at the model's pick, case 7 of the mismatch probe, which is not a library product; every library product name is overloaded (FortressLibrary.fsi:1777-1800)
- F-156 **The interpreter's library declares no reshape, plane, gather, outer product or ravel over its arrays.** A plane function takes one plane of a rank-3 array as a matrix. So a program that needs one of these declares it itself, as Run C4's vocabulary (`explorations/run-c4/src/FlatArrays.fsi`) declares `view`, `heads`, `plane`, `gather`, `onehot` and `flat`. Read by a search of the library's api. Detail: `run-c4/probes/vocabulary/REPORT.md`, question 5 and section A.
  by: the clarity pass; note: reorganized (consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/facts/f14/proposals.md, "F-156"); split: the authorship of the scalar-extension block's comment and the pointer to Maessen's commit are now F-?4; the consequence and C4's declarations from the report's question 5
- F-?4 **The comment on the library's scalar-extension block is the revival's, not the team's, so a claim of the team's intent on per-type array storage cites Maessen's commit `353818213` instead.** The comment (`Library/FortressLibrary.fsi`, above the block of F-119) says that a static checker loses a sized argument's rank and size in the block's result. The revival wrote it in September 2026 (`c2b4e2c95`, `581356f32`). The team's own statement on per-type storage is the message of `353818213`, Maessen's commit of 2008-07-28, "Immutable arrays of unboxed RR64". Read by `git blame` and `git show`. Detail: `reviews/array-design-ways.md` section 9.
  from: explorations/reviews/array-design-ways.md, "9. Found on the way"; by: the clarity pass; note: split from F-156 (consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/facts/f14/proposals.md, "F-?4"); the commits and dates as the assessment read them (fsi:2696-2701 blames to c2b4e2c95 and 581356f32; 353818213 is of 2008-07-28)
- F-157 **An array is a trait with `get` and `put`, and a view is an object that implements it over other storage; this is the language's own idiom.** A subtype of `Matrix` that overrides `mul` gets its own product through the library's `Matrix` juxtaposition and `DOT`, which forward to `mul`. It needs no api line, since walk dispatches the method on the receiver's run-time type. In two measurements under walk at `b176a4b79`, views through the library cost more: the row slice `m[i,:]` took five times as long as a six-line user view on a 64 × 16 row lift (row 298), and the library's product with a diagonal `Matrix` view took 13 times as long as a row-scaling operator at n = 16, and 54 times at n = 64 (row 299). Detail: `run-c4/probes/vocabulary/REPORT.md`, question 3 (the idiom, with row 150); `run-c4/probes/vocabulary/diag-override.md`, "Result" (the override).
  by: the clarity pass; note: reworded (consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/facts/f14/proposals.md, "F-157"); both costs bounded to their measurements, tree and sizes (rows 298 and 299, at b176a4b79); the source of the idiom cited (the vocabulary report, question 3, which cites row 150); "the library's operators" narrowed to the two that forward to mul (diag-override.md, the probe's opening paragraph)
